			  "CustomInstructions": "-- GENERAL BUSINESS CONTEXT AND DATA INTERPRETATION --\n\n* This model covers retail sales data in a star schema: one fact table (factReceiptline) and three dimensions (dimCustomer, dimProduct, dimDate).\n* Each row in factReceiptline represents one product line item on a receipt/order.\n* All monetary values (SalesAmountUSD, ProfitAmountUSD) are in US dollars.\n* When a user mentions \"sales\", \"revenue\", or \"turnover\", they are referring to the SalesAmountUSD field.\n* When a user mentions \"profit\" or \"margin\", they are referring to the ProfitAmountUSD field.\n* When a user mentions \"volume\" or \"units sold\", they are referring to the Quantity field.\n* When a user mentions \"orders\" or \"transactions\", they mean unique OrderNumber values.\n* OrderNumber identifies a full order/receipt. One order can have multiple receipt lines.\n* dimCustomer uses SCD Type 2: IsCurrent = true is the current version of a customer. ValidFrom/ValidTo track historical changes.\n* dimProduct uses SCD Type 2: IsCurrent = true is the current version of a product. ValidFrom/ValidTo track historical changes.\n* dimCustomer geographic hierarchy from high to low: CustomerCountryName → CustomerRegionName → CustomerStateName → CustomerCityName → CustomerPostalCode.\n* dimProduct hierarchy from high to low: ProductCategoryName → ProductSubCategoryName → ProductName.\n* dimDate time hierarchy from high to low: Year → QuarterName → MonthName → Day.\n* Surrogate keys (CustomerKey, ProductKey, DateKey) are for joining only — never display them to users.\n* InsertDate and UpdateDate on all tables are ETL audit fields — do not use them for business analysis.\n* The model has two date relationships: OrderDateKey (active, default) represents when the order was placed. ShipDateKey (inactive) represents when the order was shipped.\n\n-- ANALYSIS RULES --\n\n* Use the factReceiptline table as the primary source for all sales-related questions.\n* For sales and revenue analysis, always use SUM(factReceiptline[SalesAmountUSD]).\n* For profit and margin analysis, always use SUM(factReceiptline[ProfitAmountUSD]).\n* For volume analysis, always use SUM(factReceiptline[Quantity]).\n* For order count, always use DISTINCTCOUNT(factReceiptline[OrderNumber]).\n* For average order value, use DIVIDE(SUM(factReceiptline[SalesAmountUSD]), DISTINCTCOUNT(factReceiptline[OrderNumber])).\n* All time-based analysis should use dimDate via OrderDateKey by default.\n* For shipping date analysis, use USERELATIONSHIP(factReceiptline[ShipDateKey], dimDate[DateKey]).\n* For shipping lead time, calculate the difference between ShipDateKey and OrderDateKey.\n* When showing monthly trends, group by dimDate[MonthYear] and sort by dimDate[FirstDayOfMonth].\n* When showing quarterly trends, group by dimDate[Year] and dimDate[QuarterName], sort by dimDate[FirstDayOfQuarter].\n* When showing day-of-week patterns, group by dimDate[WeekDayName] and sort by dimDate[Weekday].\n* For weekly analysis, use dimDate[ISOWeekOfYear].\n* When a user asks about sales by geography, use the dimCustomer geographic hierarchy.\n* When a user asks about sales by product, use the dimProduct hierarchy.\n* When a user asks to segment customers, use dimCustomer[CustomerSegmentName].\n* For current product or customer attributes, filter to IsCurrent = true unless the user specifically asks for historical or point-in-time analysis.\n* Period boundaries are available for calculations: FirstDayOfMonth, LastDayOfMonth, FirstDayOfQuarter, LastDayOfQuarter, FirstDayOfYear, LastDayOfYear, FirstDayOfNextMonth, FirstDayOfNextYear."
			}
